A1. You can take off your hat to this one. ~ DOFF
A5. This nine could knock any team cold. ~ STRYCHNINE
A14. The unemployed. ~ IDLE
A15. A sob sister. ~ CRIER
A16. A high hat school. ~ ETON
A17. An old southern cust 'em. ~ FEUD
A18. A lady uncle. ~ AUNTY
A19. The blow that killed father. ~ TOOT
A20. Plead. ~ BEG
A21. A quint's preserver (abbr.). ~ DR
A22. Aye, here's the rub! ~ ERASE
A27. A Coney Island floater. ~ RIND
A30. Hot dog days. ~ JULY
A32. Get this one on a short wave. ~ RIPPLE
A34. This is neither either nor or. ~ NOR
A35. To get this one you may need a pick-ax, mine frand. ~ ORE
A36. Left without a head. ~ EFT
A37. This will put you to sleep. ~ DRUG
A39. Long, long ago. ~ AGES
A42. This was on the eve of the event. ~ NT
A43. What she wanted his salary to be. ~ HERS
A44. A measure that has helped to make millions. ~ MIL
A45. One thing that Huey is long on. ~ AIR
A47. Dumb belle. ~ DORA
A48. Hands off. ~ VENUS
A50. You'll probably hear this before long. ~ SO
A51. In a pinch this makes a good chaser. ~ COP
A52. An old prefix with a new meaning. ~ NEO
A54. A flowery word (plur.). ~ SEPALS
A56. This was never on time. ~ LATE
A57. Another depression in the country. ~ DALE
A59. Preserves (Scotch). ~ SAVES
A60. The head of an elephant and the tail of a camel (so what?). ~ EL
A61. This was bound to o'cur. ~ CAN
A63. A military stooge. ~ AIDE
A66. A crossword user. ~ IRATE
A68. This one is big because it has a large pop. ~ CITY
A71. This is too much on the square to take tips. ~ SCOW
A72. A tough band that plays for prize fighters. ~ SINEW
A73. The tooth, the whole tooth and nothing but the tooth. ~ ACHE
A74. These are over when they discover a nag and a mule make a poor team. ~ HONEYMOONS
A75. They yank this poor thing out of the bed very early. ~ WEED

D1. A horse of another color. ~ DIFFERENT
D2. This could be verse. ~ ODE
D3. A short spell of sickness. ~ FLU
D4. What Ante did to the kitty. ~ FED
D5. The finish of a sore subject. ~ SCAB
D6. What the girl who true him down was to the other fellow. ~ TRUER
D7. What some of our Congressmen are doing for the poor bell-hops. ~ RINGING
D8. Do not give up, you may get it eventually. ~ YET
D9. A high-pitched bawl. ~ CRY
D10. This has always been in the racket game. ~ NET
D11. A Japanese statesman. ~ ITO
D12. This soup should make good brain food. ~ NOODLE
D13. Here is where you are taken in. ~ ENTRY
D23. A bad break. ~ RIFT
D24. An abbreviated modern apartment. ~ APT
D25. This is how all spooning starts. ~ SP
D26. The oldest man of the mountains. ~ ELDER
D28. The negative side of Reno. ~ NO
D29. These are the things that made the country go dry. ~ DRAINS
D30. A penner. ~ JOE
D31. This is a bear. ~ URSA
D33. What a man usually runs in his own home. ~ ERRAND
D38. We. ~ US
D40. Stick-ups. ~ GLUES
D41. The kind of man that sees both sides of a situation. ~ CROSSEYED
D43. What old maids live in. ~ HOPE
D44. Look out! this might be you. ~ ME
D46. This must be Capri. ~ ISLE
D47. Miss Dorothea Van Dusan Jones. ~ DOT
D48. A hot-headed one that often throws rocks. ~ VOLCANO
D49. Part Spanish and part Spaghetti. ~ SPA
D51. Old-style print. ~ CALICO
D53. Another monster, the head of an eagle and the tail of a flea. ~ EA
D55. This makes a small average. ~ AV
D56. Even the dogs are on to this. ~ LEASH
D58. Mary had a little lamb and later it was this. ~ EATEN
D62. When a Scotchman gives a tip. An Englishman sees the point and Pat refuses to fight. ~ NEWS
D64. The noble kid from Spain. ~ DON
D65. A sh-sh-she-sheep. ~ EWE
D66. Here is your little dog, ma. ~ ISM
D67. What those South Americas are rowing over. ~ RIO
D68. "Quoth the raven." ~ CAW
D69. Don't slip up on this one. ~ ICE
D70. This article makes a man particular. ~ THE


